# Rust and WebAssembly Standards

## Error Handling

Use `Result<T, E>` for fallible operations. Propagate errors with `?` operator.

```rust
// ✅ GOOD
fn parse_date(input: &str) -> Result<NaiveDate, Box<dyn Error>> {
    NaiveDate::parse_from_str(input, "%Y-%m-%d")
        .map_err(|e| Box::new(e) as Box<dyn Error>)
}

// ❌ BAD
fn parse_date(input: &str) -> NaiveDate {
    NaiveDate::parse_from_str(input, "%Y-%m-%d").unwrap()
}
```

## WebAssembly Patterns

- Use `wasm-bindgen` for JavaScript interop
- Export functions with `#[wasm_bindgen]` attribute
- Use `serde` for JSON serialization (WASM-compatible)
- Avoid `std::io` in WASM code - use browser APIs via `web-sys` instead
- Keep WASM exports minimal - expose high-level calculation functions, not internal helpers

```rust
// ✅ GOOD - WASM export
#[wasm_bindgen]
pub fn calculate_rolling_absences(json_input: &str) -> Result<String, JsValue> {
    let periods: Vec<AbsencePeriod> = serde_json::from_str(json_input)
        .map_err(|e| JsValue::from_str(&format!("Parse error: {}", e)))?;
    // ... calculation logic
    serde_json::to_string(&results)
        .map_err(|e| JsValue::from_str(&format!("Serialize error: {}", e)))
}
```

## Testing

Use `#[cfg(test)]` modules. Test WASM functions separately from core logic.

```rust
#[cfg(test)]
mod tests {
    use super::*;
    // Test core logic without WASM bindings
}
```

## Documentation

Use `///` for public items, `//!` for crate-level docs. Document WASM exports clearly.

## Code Organization

Separate WASM bindings from core calculation logic. Keep core functions pure and testable.
